## Issues

_No issues found. Every desired outcome is met; no undesired outcome is tripped._

## Retro

**What the pipeline did well this cycle:**

The spec's Strategy section codified the two-idempotency-mode distinction
explicitly and named the distinguishing question ("who owns the bytes after
first write?"). This gave the implementor a concrete decision rule that
produced correct, independent code paths — no shared comparator temptation,
no accidental byte-comparison holdover.

The plan was precise about the negative space: it did not just say "remove
would-overwrite" — it called out that the old `compareBytes` import should
be removed from `provisionBrain/index.ts`, that `safeReadFile` should stay
(used by `buildObsidianMcpStep`), and that `compareBytes` itself must remain
in the shared folder for other callers. That specificity prevented both
over-deletion and under-deletion.

The parameterized regression test (`describe.each` over four on-disk states)
is the correct pattern for "this must never happen under any circumstance"
guarantees. Future changes to either seed-file builder will have to pass all
four scenarios — absent, byte-identical, drifted, completely replaced — or
the test suite fails visibly. This is the right regression anchor for a
data-loss-class invariant.

**Stage to watch going forward:**

The prior QA cycle caught fabricated wikilinks in the `## Always Read First`
section. The fix introduced placeholder wikilinks
(`[[your-conventions-note]]`, `[[your-folder-structure-note]]`) and a
negative assertion in the test. This cycle's test suite confirms those
assertions are in place and passing. The pattern to watch: any future edit to
`PLAYBOOK_HUB_TEMPLATE` that adds named wikilinks (even well-intentioned
ones) will trip the negative assertion at lines 158-160. That is intentional
— but an implementor who does not know why the test forbids `[[conventions]]`
may be tempted to remove the assertion rather than rethink the template. The
spec's `outcomes.undesired[1]` should be the first place a future implementor
looks when that assertion fails.

**Alignment confidence:**

The parent `src/tools/init/.aide` says init "pauses when a category would
overwrite an existing file." The seed-semantic update narrows this correctly:
the brain category can still produce `would-overwrite` via the MCP obsidian
sub-step (stale vault path), which is the intended canonical-template
behavior. The two seed files simply never contribute to that trigger. The
`init.md` overwrite-branch prompt (line 32: "any step is would-overwrite") is
data-driven, contains no hardcoded file names, and is naturally correct after
this change — confirmed by reading the prompt in full. No edit to `init.md`
was needed or made.
